{
 "groups": [
  [
   "greet",
   "挨拶",
   "Greetings and set phrases",
   "Fixed phrases you say as a whole: hello, thank you, sorry, and the ones around meals and leaving. Most are written in hiragana even where a kanji form exists."
  ],
  [
   "inter",
   "相づち",
   "Reactions and fillers",
   "The small words that keep a conversation going: yes, I see, well, um. Japanese listeners use them far more often than English ones do."
  ],
  [
   "question",
   "疑問詞",
   "Question words",
   "Put the question word where the answer would go and end with か: 駅はどこですか (where is the station?)."
  ],
  [
   "point",
   "こそあど",
   "Pointing words",
   "One system covers this, that and which. こ is near me, そ is near you, あ is over there, and ど asks which. Add れ for a thing, こ for a place, ちら for a direction (or politely, a person), の before a noun, and んな for \"this kind of\"."
  ],
  [
   "link",
   "接続詞",
   "Linking words",
   "Words that join a sentence to the one before it: and then, but, so, by the way. They usually come first in the sentence."
  ],
  [
   "time",
   "いつ",
   "When and how often",
   "Always, still, already, soon, at last. They usually sit near the front of the sentence, before the verb."
  ],
  [
   "degree",
   "どのくらい",
   "How much",
   "Very, a little, almost, not at all. Watch the ones that need a negative verb: あまり and ちっとも mean \"not very\" and \"not at all\" only with ～ない."
  ],
  [
   "manner",
   "どうやって",
   "How and in what spirit",
   "Certainly, properly, on purpose, in any case: words that colour how something is done or how you feel about it."
  ],
  [
   "sound",
   "擬音語",
   "Sound and feeling words",
   "Japanese uses these where English uses adverbs or vivid verbs. Many take する (びっくりする, いらいらする) or と (にっこりと)."
  ],
  [
   "desc",
   "形容",
   "Describing words",
   "い-adjectives and な-adjectives usually written in kana. い-adjectives change their ending (うるさくない); な-adjectives take な before a noun (にぎやかな町)."
  ],
  [
   "verb",
   "動詞",
   "Verbs written in kana",
   "Everyday verbs usually written in hiragana, including the giving and receiving verbs (あげる, くれる, もらう) and polite forms such as いらっしゃる and おっしゃる."
  ],
  [
   "expr",
   "表現",
   "Useful endings and expressions",
   "Phrases that hang on the end of a sentence: かもしれない (might), しょうがない (it can't be helped), もったいない (what a waste)."
  ],
